If you kept every single document you’ve ever owned on your physical desk, you’d run out of space and go bankrupt buying more desks. 
Data tiering is the automated discipline of moving data from the expensive, high-speed desk of active memory down into the cheap, deep-freeze storage of the basement as it ages.

Technical Breakdown :- 
At the physical infrastructure level, data tiering categorizes storage into 'Hot', 'Warm', and 'Cold' zones. '
Hot' data—like yesterday's transactions—lives on premium, millisecond-latency storage like NVMe SSDs or AWS S3 Standard. 
As data cools over 30 to 90 days, automated Lifecycle Policies transition it to 'Warm' storage like S3 Infrequent Access. 
Finally, data retained purely for multi-year regulatory compliance is archived into 'Cold' storage like S3 Glacier Deep Archive, 
where storage costs drop by up to 95%, but data retrieval takes hours instead of milliseconds.

Buisness Context :- 
Storage costs are compounding liabilities. If a company retains 5 petabytes of legacy telemetry logs on premium hot storage for five years, 
they are burning millions of dollars annually for data nobody is querying. Data tiering mathematically aligns the infrastructure cost of a dataset with its actual,
present-day business value, slashing cloud storage bills by up to 80% without deleting a single record.

Design Decision :- 
Some engineers often build rigid, time-based lifecycle rules—like blindly archiving everything older than 90 days—which catastrophically backfires 
when a data scientist suddenly needs to run a historical ML training job, triggering massive 'data retrieval' penalty fees from Glacier. 
As a data engineer, I leverage machine-learning-driven tiering, like AWS S3 Intelligent-Tiering. It actively monitors query access patterns at the object level, 
automatically moving unused data to colder tiers, and instantly snapping it back to hot storage the millisecond it's queried, perfectly optimizing costs with zero manual 
intervention or retrieval penalties.
